          {
            ":db/id": 17592186158168,
            ":step/content": {
              ":db/id": 17592186158462,
              ":tutorial/content": "Another way to plot $x = f(y)$ is to first plot the corresponding function $y = f(x)$ and then reflect it over the line $y=x$.\n\nTo illustrate, let's use this technique to plot the curve $x=y^2$. To get the corresponding function, we swap $x$ and $y$ and get $y=x^2$. This curve is familiar and much easier to graph:\n\n![](image_assets/61a79246c46a57d8b1aa3d107206fdc6-afca2357f9.png)\n\nNow, to get the curve $x=y^2$, we just have to reflect the curve $y=x^2$ over the line $y=x$, as follows:\n\n![](image_assets/04afecb8450c42675b6307b61aacc623-5b91959bd8.png)\n\nYou may notice that this process is very similar to plotting the inverse of a function. In fact, if $y=f(x)$ is a one-to-one function, then the corresponding function $x = f(y)$ will always give the inverse function.",
              ":tutorial/id": "ef9c9a8e-f3c3-57cf-936a-e4586282e650",
              ":tutorial/math-academy-id": 4708,
              ":tutorial/title": "Plotting X as a Function of Y Using a Reflection"
            },
            ":step/id": "fe2be374-df26-589e-a401-2e869139d14d",
            ":step/math-academy-id": 29075,
            ":step/next": {
